KNOWNBUG libstdcxx
main.cpp
--cpp17 --object-bits 12
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ variant alternative: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
A probe of the C++17 support census (2026-11-05): std::variant holding a
double.  The last assertion is WRONG and must fail, so a pass is not vacuous.

With libstdc++ 11's <variant>, initializing variant<int, double> from a
double was rejected ("found no match for symbol 'variant'"): its
__tuple_count chose the wrong partial specialization
(cpp11_partial_specialization_nested_pack_empty).
